st or ovarian cancer\" frame appears elsewhere in this abstract without a True Positive annotation, which is consistent with the same syntactic rule. I could only verify one discrepancy, so the rule is supported by cross-document contrast rather than by a full set of discrepancy examples.\n\n**Step 2: Pattern Insight**\n\n- **Pattern Name:** Disease Term as Attributive Premodifier vs. Referential NP-Final Head in a Prepositional Complement\n- **Confusion Trigger:** The disease string \"ovarian cancer\" is a lexically frequent Modifier in this document, and the model over-weights string identity and the adjacent \"ovarian cancer risk\" context. The \"family history of [disease]\" frame encourages reading the disease as a qualifier of \"history,\" so the model labels the span Modifier even though it is the NP-final head of an \"of\"-complement that refers to the disease entity.\n- **Contrastive Evidence:** Every True Positive Modifier has the disease term followed inside the same NP by a non-disease head noun it modifies (cases, population, study, risk, patients, genes, protein). The discrepancy has the disease term as the rightmost noun before a clause boundary (a comma), serving as the object of \"of.\" This matches the SpecificDisease pattern in \"patients **with** SLS\" and \"Coats disease is characterized…,\" where the disease term stands as a referent rather than a premodifier.\n- **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):**\n  1. Label the disease span **Modifier** only if it is immediately followed, within the same noun phrase, by a non-disease head noun that it attributively modifies (e.g., cases, population, study, risk, patients, genes, protein).\n  2. Label it **SpecificDisease** if the span is the NP-final head, the complement of \"of,\" \"with,\" or a similar preposition, or a subject or object that refers to the disease entity itself, even when the preceding context is \"family history,\" \"risk,\" or \"patients.\"\n  3. Do not use repetition of the same string else